31	%=============================================================================
32	\begin{abstract}
33	%=============================================================================
34	Benchmark contamination---the inadvertent inclusion of test data in training corpora---undermines the reliability of LLM evaluation, yet existing detection methods stop at flagging without providing calibrated scores. We introduce \contamcal{}, a two-stage framework that bridges contamination \emph{detection} and score \emph{calibration}. Stage~1 computes per-item exposure scores by fusing four heterogeneous detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). Stage~2 feeds these exposure scores into a contamination-augmented 4PL Item Response Theory (IRT) model with per-benchmark contamination effect parameters $\gamma_k$, producing calibrated benchmark scores with Bayesian credible intervals. Through controlled SFT contamination injection across Qwen2.5 models (0.5B--7B) on four benchmarks (ARC, GSM8K, MMLU, HumanEval), we establish three contributions: (1)~\emph{Benchmark-dependent calibration}---contamination effects are heterogeneous across benchmarks ($\gamma_{\text{ARC}}=-1.25$, $\gamma_{\text{GSM8K}}=+2.85$), and \contamcal{} calibrates in both directions, reducing ARC MAE from 0.059 to 0.035; (2)~\emph{Multi-scale signal effectiveness}---detection signal reliability is scale-dependent, with Min-K++ exhibiting directional reversal at small scales (Cohen's $d=-1.98$ at 0.5B) but recovering at 1.5B+ (AUC$\geq$0.68); (3)~\emph{Identifiability bounds}---Fisher Information analysis establishes structural identifiability at signal AUC$\geq$0.55 and practical identifiability at AUC$\geq$0.70. An observational RL case study demonstrates 33\% reduction in model selection regret, while cross-family validation (Llama-3.2-1B) confirms that contamination susceptibility is a benchmark property (cross-family $\gamma$ direction correlation $r=0.88$).
35	\end{abstract}
36	
37	%=============================================================================
38	\section{Introduction}
39	\label{sec:intro}
40	%=============================================================================
41	
42	Benchmark contamination has become a central threat to the reliability of LLM evaluation. When test items appear in training data---whether through direct inclusion, paraphrasing, or data pipeline leakage---benchmark scores become inflated, and model comparisons become unreliable. Recent work has demonstrated that rephrased test items can inflate MMLU accuracy by 8--15 points while evading standard $n$-gram decontamination~\citep{yang2023rethinking}, and a comprehensive study of 20 mitigation strategies found none to be reliably effective~\citep{empcloths2025}.
43	
44	A growing ecosystem of detection methods can identify contaminated items: embedding similarity~\citep{golchin2024time}, Min-K\%++~\citep{zhang2024minkpp}, ConStat~\citep{dekoninck2024constat}, and Self-Critique~\citep{weller2024selfcritique}. However, these methods stop at detection---they flag contamination but do not answer the fundamental evaluation question: \emph{what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?}
45	
46	We argue that the appropriate response to contamination is not elimination (which provably fails) but \emph{statistical calibration}---analogous to causal inference's shift from eliminating confounders to statistically controlling for them. This insight connects to a well-established tradition in educational measurement, where item compromise IRT models~\citep{eckerly2017detecting, sinharay2017assessment} handle leaked exam items by modeling exposure as a covariate in the response function.
47	
48	We introduce \contamcal{}, a two-stage framework that bridges contamination detection and benchmark calibration:
49	
50	\begin{itemize}
51	\item \textbf{Stage 1: Multi-signal exposure mapping.} Four detection signals (embedding similarity, Min-K++, ConStat, Self-Critique) are computed per item and fused via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)~\citep{cormack2009reciprocal} into a composite exposure score. RRF is training-free by design, avoiding meta-circularity where the aggregator's training data might itself be contaminated.
52	
53	\item \textbf{Stage 2: Contamination-augmented 4PL IRT.} The exposure scores are incorporated as covariates in a four-parameter logistic IRT model~\citep{deboeck2004explanatory} with per-benchmark contamination effect parameters $\gamma_k$, producing calibrated scores with Bayesian credible intervals via MCMC inference.
54	\end{itemize}
55	
56	Through systematic characterization of contamination effects across model scales, benchmarks, and detection signals, we make three core contributions:
57	
58	\begin{enumerate}
59	\item \textbf{Benchmark-dependent calibration.} Contamination effects are not uniformly inflationary: ARC exhibits forgetting ($\gamma<0$) while GSM8K shows inflation ($\gamma>0$). \contamcal{} calibrates in both directions, achieving MAE of 0.035 on ARC versus 0.059 for raw scores.
60	
61	\item \textbf{Multi-scale signal effectiveness.} Detection signal reliability is method-specific and scale-dependent. Min-K++ fails at 0.5B (AUC$\approx$0.47) but recovers at 1.5B (AUC=0.68) and 3B (AUC=0.74). Critically, Min-K++ exhibits \emph{directional reversal} at high contamination dosages in small models (Cohen's $d=-1.98$), actively misleading rather than merely failing.
62	
63	\item \textbf{Identifiability bounds with Fisher Information analysis.} We derive Fisher Information for $\gamma$ in the 4PL+$\gamma$ model, establishing structural identifiability at signal AUC$\geq$0.55 and practical identifiability at AUC$\geq$0.70, with $\text{CI width} \propto N^{-0.48}$ ($R^2=0.97$).
64	\end{enumerate}
65	
66	%=============================================================================
67	\section{Related Work}
68	\label{sec:related}
69	%=============================================================================
70	
71	\paragraph{Contamination detection.}
72	A suite of methods detect benchmark contamination at item or dataset level. Min-K\%++~\citep{zhang2024minkpp} uses token-level perplexity statistics as membership inference signals, extending the MIA framework~\citep{shokri2017membership, carlini2022extracting}. ConStat~\citep{dekoninck2024constat} compares performance on contaminated vs.\ clean subsets. Self-Critique~\citep{weller2024selfcritique} prompts models to assess their own familiarity. Embedding-based methods~\citep{golchin2024time} measure semantic similarity between training and test items. KDS~\citep{dong2024kds} and CDD~\citep{shi2024cdd} provide complementary approaches. All these methods flag contamination but produce no calibrated scores.
73	
74	\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
75	Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation for ability estimation~\citep{lalor2024aaai}, benchmark efficiency~\citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}, and psychometric profiling~\citep{burnell2023multidimensional}. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty quantification but ignores contamination. Growing Pains~\citep{raju2024growingpains} uses IRT to study evaluation dynamics. None of these approaches model contamination as a covariate in the IRT framework.
76	
77	\paragraph{Item compromise in educational measurement.}
78	Our work is grounded in the psychometric tradition of item compromise detection~\citep{eckerly2017detecting, sinharay2017assessment}, which models leaked exam items via differential item functioning (DIF)~\citep{holland1993dif, thissen1993dif, zumbo1999dif}. We adapt this framework with three structural modifications for LLM evaluation: independent examinees $\to$ single model with joint item distribution, binary leakage $\to$ continuous exposure scores, and stable ability $\to$ regime-conditional ability.
79	
80	\paragraph{Contamination mitigation.}
81	DCR~\citep{oren2024dcr} provides fuzzy-matched adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty quantification and RL handling. Emperor's New Clothes~\citep{empcloths2025} demonstrates that rephrasing and option shuffling---representative mitigation strategies---are ineffective. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench~\citep{livebench2024}) and human evaluation (Chatbot Arena~\citep{zheng2023chatbot}) sidestep contamination but require continuous curation.
82	
83	\paragraph{Calibration disambiguation.}
84	We distinguish our use of ``calibration'' (decontamination-based score adjustment) from probability calibration~\citep{guo2017calibration, platt1999probabilistic} and from conformal prediction~\citep{angelopoulos2023conformal}, which provides distribution-free coverage guarantees. We adopt Bayesian credible intervals for their natural integration with the IRT generative model and informative prior specification.
85	
86	%=============================================================================
87	\section{Method}
88	\label{sec:method}
89	%=============================================================================
90	
91	\subsection{Overview}
92	
93	\contamcal{} operates in two stages. Stage~1 computes per-item contamination exposure scores by aggregating multiple detection signals. Stage~2 incorporates these scores into a contamination-augmented IRT model to produce calibrated benchmark scores with uncertainty quantification.
94	
